136
00:10:19,600 --> 00:10:21,440
They're like, it just feels like it's going faster.

137
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:24,320
And it's like, okay, like you can still lie to yourself really easily.

138
00:10:24,480 --> 00:10:25,600
So what did that look like for you?

139
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:35,360
Yeah, no, I could not agree more and could go on a lot and a lot of rants about this or just in general, like, you know, benchmarks or anything like that.

140
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:41,920
Um, I think it's, you know, and I'll just speak for myself because, like you said, it's like totally different for every company.

141
00:10:42,560 --> 00:10:49,840
Um, but the, the first signs is when I remember I was working the first and only job I had after college.

142
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:51,920
I was a system engineer at Progressive Insurance.

143
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:56,080
So I was in their data center, literally like working on servers, had no idea what I was doing.

144
00:10:56,400 --> 00:10:58,240
But I was there for like six months.

145
00:10:58,320 --> 00:11:03,440
And I remember I was like at work searching Twitter, like rap chat on Twitter, just a term.

146
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:11,360
And then over time, like more and more people just kept sharing their tracks to Twitter and like saying how much they love it.

147
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:14,320
And then app store reviews were a big thing.

148
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:18,720
I mean, it was just clear that we like, like people truly loved the product.

149
00:11:20,320 --> 00:11:24,480
And that was kind of the first step and you can't really quantify.

150
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:28,160
It's not like, oh, there was a thousand Twitter, you know, quotes or something.

151
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:31,600
You weren't measuring like day one retention, day thirty retention.

152
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:32,160
I was.

153
00:11:32,480 --> 00:11:34,400
I learned, I learned all that stuff over time.

154
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:36,720
And like, we track, we track a lot of that stuff.

155
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:41,040
But I'm telling you, like, the most important stuff was like the qualitative in the early days.

156
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:44,800
Um, and then, but you need qualitative at scale.

157
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:51,200
Like, it's not just like your friend, you know, it's like, oh, plus, you know, fifty thousand MAUs at that time or whatever it was.

158
00:11:51,840 --> 00:11:54,640
And I think that was really key.

159
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:59,760
Like the first thing is like people were actually able to record music on their phone and share it.

160
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:02,080
Some people were really good at it.

161
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:05,520
Like this is kind of like obvious now, but it wasn't back in the day.

162
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:17,840
Like there was like technical challenges, there were, you know, people didn't think it would be a thing, some people still don't think it's like a billion opportunity, but like, you know, we had to prove that people would really record music on their phone.

163
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:19,920
Like that was, it seemed so obvious.

164
00:12:20,320 --> 00:12:21,680
What was the Propeller Heads app?

165
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:24,160
Gosh, what was that called?

166
00:12:24,400 --> 00:12:25,280
They've had a few, I think.

167
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,480
There was, there was, I remember, that's bad podcasting.

168
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:34,880
I don't know the name of it, but I remember there was some really key like music apps that were kind of around that era, right?

169
00:12:35,040 --> 00:12:41,680
It's like the phones are finally getting fast enough to be able to do this without like just falling over and dying in like 1940 time frame.

170
00:12:41,840 --> 00:12:45,360
And Android's still like really freaking hard to get right.

171
00:12:45,680 --> 00:12:52,880
But I mean, over time, now we have like a process of like how we think about, you know, does our product work for a certain market?

172
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:53,760
And it's changed.

173
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:58,240
Like I would say, you never really, we're not in a desert, but you never stop wandering.

174
00:12:58,400 --> 00:12:59,920
Like your product evolves over time.

175
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:01,760
The market evolves over time.

176
00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:06,800
We've seen different personas evolve and grow in our community over time.

177
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:17,760
And now, like I said in the early days, a lot of it was providing just like a fun, uh, social media app that was music based for the for everyone.

178
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:28,240
And now while we do have those components, it's much more about providing a really great recording studio in your pocket with a community of creators for the everyday artists.

179
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:42,560
Like, so now we've actually, you know, zoned in a little bit more and focused on one or two specific segments and we have really strong metrics engagement, uh, now subscriptions for that specific persona.

180
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:44,080
So I think that's a big thing.

181
00:13:44,240 --> 00:13:51,840
Like in the early days, you read all these blogs and, uh, you know, what to look at for retention or what to look at for product market fit.

182
00:13:52,240 --> 00:13:55,520
But a lot of times it's not married with context of like personas.

183
00:13:55,760 --> 00:14:08,640
And so for the first three years, I mean, we're getting whatever, millions of downloads a year, but like this person in India is here to have fun, this person in Georgia is here to take it seriously, and we were just looking at it all blended.

184
00:14:09,440 --> 00:14:14,160
And so like once we learn to actually be like, no, like now we literally ask like, why are you here?

185
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:15,440
Like, what are your aspirations?

186
00:14:16,880 --> 00:14:18,480
And then we view things through that lens.

187
00:14:18,640 --> 00:14:20,000
That's been one of the biggest unlocks.

188
00:14:20,480 --> 00:14:30,640
Like, it seems obvious again, but if you don't think that way, then in the early days you're just kind of like, wandering, like, well, why is my day one retention like not changing?

189
00:14:30,880 --> 00:14:37,040
It's like, well, you know, you're getting thirty percent of your users from this like really bad channel and they're low intent.

190
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:47,280
And like, you should filter those out because that's noise, you know, I it's so tricky though because I I was in a similar position when we were trying to work on growth to elevate.

191
00:14:47,520 --> 00:14:53,600
And, um, you can, you can really easily that thing where founders are trying to lie to themselves.

192
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:55,360
It's a very easy way to lie to yourself.

193
00:14:56,080 --> 00:15:00,400
And be like, yeah, I have a great retention if I just ignore all the users with bad retention, right?

194
00:15:00,640 --> 00:15:02,000
And it's like, yeah, yeah.

195
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:04,080
I think context is the important thing, right?

196
00:15:04,240 --> 00:15:06,560
It's like, okay, like what is the actual context for this?

197
00:15:06,720 --> 00:15:12,400
And I mean, it makes me think of the photo room founder who we had on a couple, a couple weeks ago.

198
00:15:13,760 --> 00:15:23,200
I don't know, ordering the podcast comes down, but they also had a similar situation where they found a, like, within their greater user base, like a persona where retention was really strong, intent was really high.

199
00:15:23,840 --> 00:15:41,840
And then it's kind of great because it gives you, I feel like from a founder and product perspective, it gives you license to focus right a little bit and be like, okay, like we found this profile that's going to be our most important and and we're going to like really put our energy here and it kind of clarifies a lot of like things for the product decision making.

200
00:15:42,320 --> 00:16:00,480
One thing to interject on this real quick is that, um, I think a lot of people underestimate just how amazing Facebook got at doing this for founders because that the feedback loop and and and Facebook's algorithm and how much data they had on people prior to abstract.

